<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Avicenna</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 980–<a href="/years/1037">1037</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Ibn Sina, Abu Ali al-Husayn ibn Sina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Avicenna, Ibn Sina, was a Persian philosopher and physician born near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-canon-of-medicine">Canon of Medicine</a> was a standard medical textbook in the Islamic world and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for centuries, and his Book of Healing shaped later philosophy. He served several courts, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gurganj">Gurganj</a> and the Kakuyid ruler Ala al-Dawla at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, where he was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ibn Sina</b> served the Buyid emir Shams al-Dawla of Hamadan as vizier, and al-Sahib ibn Abbad gathered scholars, including the Mu'tazili theologian Abd al-Jabbar, at Ray.</blockquote><a href="/empire/buyid-dynasty">Buyid dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…al-Andalus to fall, and in Toledo, where Archbishop Raymond (1125–1152) encouraged the work, scholars such as Gerard of Cremona and Dominicus Gundissalinus translated Arabic works of philosophy, astronomy and medicine into Latin, including Ptolemy's Almagest and works of <b>Avicenna</b> and al-Farabi, transmitting them to the schools of Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The theologians debated God’s attributes and human free will; the philosophers, among them al-Farabi, <b>Ibn Sina</b> and Ibn Rushd, drew on Greek thought; and the Sufis sought direct, loving experience of God through prayer, music and meditation, organised from the 12th century in brotherhoods across the Muslim world.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Al-Khwarizmi’s book on algebra, written in Baghdad around 820, gave the subject its name; Ibn al-Haytham’s Book of Optics transformed the study of light; and the Canon of Medicine of <b>Ibn Sina</b> and the commentaries of Ibn Rushd on Aristotle, translated into Latin, shaped European learning for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/language/arabic">Arabic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Dante's Europe also had substantial contact with the Muslim world, through Averroism and the patronage of Alfonso X of Castile; Frederick Copleston argued in 1950 that Dante's respect for Averroes and <b>Avicenna</b> acknowledges a “considerable debt” to Islamic philosophy.</blockquote><a href="/work/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The commentaries of the Andalusian philosopher Ibn Rushd, known in Latin as Averroes, shaped the reading of Aristotle in the universities of medieval Europe, and the Canon of Medicine of Ibn Sina, or <b>Avicenna</b>, was taught in European medical schools for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Afshona, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.99,64.38&amp;year=1017" aria-label="Afshona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6135 733 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Avicenna"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6585 1003h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6585 1003h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.99,64.38&amp;year=1017">What was here in 1017? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
